raye-base (0.5~beta1) resolute; urgency=medium

  * The system now calls itself Raye Linux 0.5 Beta Alpenglow.
  * Windows programs run on Raye's own Windows layer, with .NET, the web
    engine some programs embed and DirectX through Vulkan. The outside
    packages and package source used for them in 0.3 are removed on
    upgrade.
  * Windows programs: games blocked by their anti-cheat stop before the
    installer runs, WebView2 sign-in pages no longer crash, profiles
    match programs saved in Downloads, VST plugins go into FL Studio's
    space, programs open the files and links their profile names,
    uninstallers with spaces in their path work, and a space is brought
    up to the current Raye settings before it runs.
  * The .deb of minecraft.net installs.
  * Crash details and Windows components say root, not administrator.
  * unattended-upgrades leaves the desktop and boot packages to Raye
    Update, with every binary of the sources Raye Update keeps for a
    restart (Kirigami, the NSS modules, locales...), and Raye's Plasma
    builds are pinned above Ubuntu updates.
  * The boot menu has a "Raye Linux, earlier states" entry to start from
    a restore point, named like in Settings and in French too, without
    the after-update points; going back at boot talks to the boot screen
    through its own keys.
  * The last three restore points made by hand are kept out of update
    cleanup.
  * Boot failures show again, and an ended session brings back the sign-
    in screen instead of a text console.
  * avahi-utils is pulled in for the Device Portal, and Raye Archive
    replaces Ark.
  * System Monitor pins from 0.2 become Task Manager pins at sign-in.
  * Typing notepad, regedit, taskmgr, control or cmd in a terminal opens
    the Raye app for it; winetricks is no longer a command anyone sees.
  * Windows programs print nothing in a terminal and never make ~/.wine;
    a ~/.wine left by 0.3 becomes the default space, nothing lost.
  * Every Windows program gets a default profile, WinRAR a checked one,
    and a slow first setup shows "Preparing <program>…".
  * A shortcut starts its program in the program's own folder.
  * "Preparing <program>…" stays up until the program starts, then goes
    away instead of lingering in the notification list.
  * Microsoft's Visual C++ runtimes 2005 to 2022 and the core fonts are
    fetched in the background after boot and put in every new Windows
    space, with nothing on screen. A program no longer fetches again what
    its space already has, and Paint.NET brings its own .NET.
  * Programs load Microsoft's Visual C++ runtimes from the ready space
    instead of the layer's own copies. The ready space is rebuilt as soon
    as new runtimes arrive, and a first boot without network tries again
    every 30 minutes instead of 6 hours.
  * Faster start: network and SAN drivers leave the initramfs (99 to 67
    MB), the graphics driver check no longer waits for the network on
    every start, binfmt_misc is not mounted for Python alone, and
    "Working on updates" shows as soon as the disk is open.
  * The TPM binding is only suspended when a Secure Boot database really
    changed, so a dbx update turned down by the firmware no longer leaves
    the disk opening without PCR 7 every other start.
  * Disk encryption can be turned on or off from Settings while the PC is
    running (raye-encryption, an initramfs hook and a polkit action); the
    plain disk is then mounted by UUID and the boot menu refreshed.
  * Game mode starts the lavd scheduler while a game runs and stops it
    afterwards; raye-gaming pulls in raye-scx.
  * raye-kernel: Settings knows at every start whether the security chip
    opens the disk, and right after the disk is encrypted from Settings.

