raye-base (0.1~dev4) resolute; urgency=medium

  * raye-system pulls the Raye boot menu theme and whiptail.
  * raye-desktop adds fastfetch and Cascadia Code.
  * raye-desktop installs Raye Settings.
  * Boot menu entry reads "Raye GNU/Linux" instead of "Raye Linux GNU/Linux".
  * Security updates install on their own (Ubuntu security and Firefox)
    until Raye Update exists. The origins are spelled out: the stock
    ${distro_id} pattern turns into "Raye" here and matches nothing.
  * apt takes a btrfs snapshot before and after each change, set up by
    raye-snapshots.service on first boot.
  * raye-drivers installs the recommended NVIDIA driver with its signed
    modules, at install or at the first boot with a network. Unsigned
    (DKMS) drivers are skipped, OEM packages never pulled in.
  * clevis in the base set, for TPM unlocking of encrypted disks.
  * raye-desktop ships Raye Update.
  * raye-desktop ships the Raye Store.
  * raye-gaming: Wine, GameMode, MangoHud. raye-windows-apps opens .exe
    and .msi files with a double click, one Wine prefix per program.
  * zram (half the RAM, zstd), BBR and fq, vm.max_map_count for games.
  * After a kernel crash the computer restarts on its own after 30 s.
  * Windows program profiles: known installers get the components they
    need and one shared space with the installed program (FL Studio,
    Notepad++, 7-Zip, Paint.NET, Battle.net). For Steam, Epic, Discord,
    Spotify, OBS, VLC and Chrome the Linux version is offered instead.
  * raye-drivers --again and raye-drivers-check.service, which the sudo
    group may start from Raye Settings without a password.

